# What Is An Occupier Waiver Form?

## Summary

When you’re buying a property with a mortgage, you might come across something called an occupier waiver form. This legal document can seem a bit daunting, but it’s a common part of many property transactions. In this article, we’ll break down what an occupier waiver form is, why it’s necessary, and how it impacts you if you’re asked to sign one. We’ll also provide practical advice on seeking independent legal advice to make sure you fully understand the implications before you sign. A typical occupier waiver form includes: * Personal details of the occupier (full name, address, and date of birth); * Information about the mortgage, including the lender’s name and borrower’s name; * A section where the occupier acknowledges that they are aware of the mortgage and lender’s rights over the property; * A statement where the occupier agrees to waive any legal right to remain in the property if the mortgage lender needs to repossess it; * A statement where the occupier waives any equitable interest in the property; * A section confirming that the occupier has received independent legal advice; * Information about the solicitor who provided the advice; * Space for the occupier to sign; *.

## Key Information

- Personal details of the occupier (full name, address, and date of birth);
- Information about the mortgage, including the lender’s name and borrower’s name;
- A section where the occupier acknowledges that they are aware of the mortgage and lender’s rights over the property;
- A statement where the occupier agrees to waive any legal right to remain in the property if the mortgage lender needs to repossess it;
- A statement where the occupier waives any equitable interest in the property;
- A section confirming that the occupier has received independent legal advice;
- Information about the solicitor who provided the advice;
- Space for the occupier to sign;
- Space for a witness to sign.
- You are buying a property and going to live it with your partner or spouse but your name isn't on the mortgage or the title deed;
- The property where you live is being remortgaged and you're not named on the mortgage or title;
- Equity release is being considered.

## Practical Guidance

1. Personal details of the occupier (full name, address, and date of birth);
2. Information about the mortgage, including the lender’s name and borrower’s name;
3. A section where the occupier acknowledges that they are aware of the mortgage and lender’s rights over the property;
4. A statement where the occupier agrees to waive any legal right to remain in the property if the mortgage lender needs to repossess it;
5. A statement where the occupier waives any equitable interest in the property;
6. A section confirming that the occupier has received independent legal advice;
7. Information about the solicitor who provided the advice;
8. Space for the occupier to sign;
9. An occupier consent form is the same as an occupier waiver form in that it is still designed to protect the mortgage lender by confirming that the occupier consents to the mortgage terms and understands that they have no right to remain if the property is repossessed.
10. The only difference is semantics. ‘Waiver’ emphasises the act of giving up rights, while ‘consent’ highlights agreement to the mortgage terms.
11. If you’re living in a property with your partner or spouse, but your name isn’t on the mortgage or the title deed, you’ll need to sign an occupier waiver form.
12. This includes situations where one partner owns the property and the other simply lives there.
